==================================================
96. DURABLE EXECUTION INVARIANTS
==================================================

The following are canonical:

1. Every significant durable execution must have an identity.

2. Execution state must be explicit.

3. UNKNOWN must remain UNKNOWN.

4. Execution state must not automatically become Memory.

5. Execution state must not automatically become Knowledge.

6. Execution must have a recoverable source of truth.

7. Critical execution steps must support verification.

8. Retried side-effect operations must define idempotency
   semantics.

9. Recovery must preserve the last valid execution state.

10. Long-running execution must support checkpoints where
    appropriate.

11. Authorization must remain valid throughout sensitive
    execution.

12. Privacy restrictions must survive execution recovery.

13. Security restrictions must survive execution recovery.

14. Fallback must not silently expand data exposure.

15. Fallback must not silently expand authorization.

16. Device failure must not automatically destroy Task identity.

17. Worker failure must not automatically destroy durable state.

18. Execution History must remain distinguishable from user
    Memory.

19. Partial execution must be represented accurately.

20. False SUCCESS is prohibited.

21. Durable execution must integrate with Recovery.

22. Durable execution must integrate with Event Model.

23. Durable execution must integrate with Observability.

24. Durable execution must remain compatible with Extensibility.

25. Execution must prioritize correctness and integrity over
    blind continuation.


